.TH AWK 1 2026-10-27 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
awk \- pattern scanning and text processing language
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B awk
.RB [ \-F
.IR fs ]
.RB [ \-v
\fIvar\fB=\fIvalue\fR]...\&
.I program
.RI [ operand ...]
.br
.B awk
.RB [ \-F
.IR fs ]
.RB [ \-v
\fIvar\fB=\fIvalue\fR]...\&
.RB { \-f
.I progfile
.RB "| " \-e
.IR text }...\&
.RI [ operand ...]
.br
.B awk
.RB [ \-F
.IR fs ]
.RB [ \-v
\fIvar\fB=\fIvalue\fR]...\&
.B \-E
.I progfile
.RI [ file ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B awk
reads text as a sequence of records, normally lines, splits each record
into fields, and runs a program of pattern-action rules on each record.
This is the busybox
.BR awk ,
which implements the POSIX language with some gawk extensions.
.PP
The program is the first operand, unless it comes from
.BR \-f ,
.BR \-e ,
or
.BR \-E .
Each remaining operand of the form
\fIvar\fB=\fIvalue\fR,
where
.I var
is a variable name, assigns
.I value
to
.I var
when
.B awk
reaches it in the list of operands, after the
.B BEGIN
rules have run.
Any other operand names an input file, and the files are read in order.
An operand of
.BR \- ,
or the absence of file operands, means standard input.
Output goes to standard output unless redirected.
.SS Program structure
A program is a sequence of rules and function definitions:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
\fIpattern\fR { \fIstatements\fR }
function \fIname\fR(\fIparameter\fR, ...) { \fIstatements\fR }
.EE
.RE
.PP
For each record,
.B awk
runs the statements of every rule whose pattern matches, in program
order.
A rule without a pattern matches every record, and a pattern without an
action prints the record.
The patterns are:
.TP
.B BEGIN
Matches once, before any input is read.
A program with only
.B BEGIN
rules exits after running them, without reading input.
.TP
.B END
Matches once, after the input is exhausted or an
.B exit
statement runs.
.BR $0 ,
.BR NF ,
and
.B NR
keep their values from the last record.
.TP
.I expression
Matches when the expression is true: a nonzero number, or a non-empty
string.
A regular expression constant alone, as in
.BR /x/ ,
means
.BR "$0 ~ /x/" .
.TP
\fIpattern1\fB, \fIpattern2\fR
Matches every record from one that matches
.I pattern1
through the next that matches
.IR pattern2 ,
inclusive, and then starts looking for
.I pattern1
again.
.PP
There may be several
.B BEGIN
and
.B END
rules, run in program order, and each needs its opening brace on the same
line.
.PP
Statements are separated by newlines or semicolons and grouped with
braces.
A newline may also follow a comma, a brace, a semicolon, or a binary
operator such as
.BR && .
A backslash at the end of a line joins it to the next, and
.B #
starts a comment that runs to the end of the line.
.PP
.B func
is a synonym for
.BR function .
Parameters are local to the function.
A caller may pass fewer arguments than there are parameters, and the
rest serve as local variables.
Scalars are passed by value and arrays by reference.
In a call, the
.B (
must follow the name immediately.
Functions may be recursive and may be called before their definition.
.SS Values
Every value is a string or a double-precision number, and is converted
as needed.
An uninitialized variable is both 0 and the empty string.
.PP
Numeric constants are decimal, with an optional fraction and exponent,
hexadecimal with a
.B 0x
prefix, or octal with a leading 0.
Octal applies only to constants in the program.
.PP
String constants are enclosed in double quotes and recognize the escapes
.BR \e\(dq ,
.BR \e\e ,
.BR \e/ ,
.BR \ea ,
.BR \eb ,
.BR \ee ,
.BR \ef ,
.BR \en ,
.BR \er ,
.BR \et ,
.BR \ev ,
.B \e
followed by 1 to 3 octal digits, and
.B \ex
followed by 1 or 2 hex digits.
A backslash before any other character is dropped.
The same escapes apply to the values of
.B \-v
and operand assignments, and to the argument of
.BR \-F .
.PP
Fields, the values read by
.BR getline ,
the elements of
.B ARGV
and
.B ENVIRON
and of arrays made by
.BR split ,
and values assigned on the command line are strings, and each one that
looks like a number, ignoring leading and trailing blanks, is also
numeric.
Two values compare as numbers when both are numeric, and otherwise as
strings, byte by byte.
A string used as a number takes the value of its longest numeric prefix,
so
.B \(dq12abc\(dq
is 12 and
.B \(dqabc\(dq
is 0.
.PP
A number with an integer value, of any size, converts to a string as an
integer; see
.BR NOTES .
Other numbers convert with the format in
.BR CONVFMT ,
or in
.B OFMT
when written by
.BR print .
.PP
Arrays are associative and indexed by strings.
The subscript
.B a[i, j]
is
.I i
and
.I j
joined by
.BR SUBSEP .
.SS Expressions
The operators, from highest to lowest precedence:
.TP
.BI ( expr )
Grouping.
.TP
.BI $ expr
Field reference.
.TP
.BR ++ " " \-\-
Increment and decrement, as a prefix or postfix.
.TP
.BR ^ " " **
Exponentiation.
.TP
.BR ! " " + " " \-
Logical not, unary plus, and negation.
.TP
.BR * " " / " " %
Multiplication, division, and remainder.
The remainder has the sign of the dividend, and division or remainder by
zero is a fatal error.
.TP
.BR + " " \-
Addition and subtraction.
.TP
.I "expr expr"
String concatenation.
.TP
.BR < " " <= " " != " " == " " > " " >=
Comparison, giving 1 or 0.
.TP
.BR ~ " " !~
Regular expression match and non-match.
The right operand is a regular expression.
.TP
.B in
.IB subscript " in " array
is 1 if
.I array
has an element with that subscript, without creating one.
A parenthesized list, as in
.BR "(i, j) in a" ,
is a multiple subscript.
.TP
.B &&
Logical and, evaluating its right operand only when needed.
.TP
.B ||
Logical or, likewise.
.TP
.B ?:
Conditional.
.TP
.BR = " " += " " \-= " " *= " " /= " " %= " " ^= " " **=
Assignment, grouping right to left.
.PP
Binary operators other than assignment group left to right.
.SS Regular expressions
Regular expressions are POSIX extended regular expressions, with the GNU
operators
.B \e<
and
.B \e>
for the start and end of a word,
.B \eb
for a word boundary,
.B \eB
for a position that is not a word boundary,
.B \ew
and
.B \eW
for word and non-word characters,
.B \es
and
.B \eS
for space and non-space characters, and
.B \e\(ga
and
.B \e\(aq
for the start and end of the string.
.PP
A regular expression constant is written between slashes.
The escapes of string constants are processed in it, except that a
backslash before any other character is kept, so
.B \e.\&
matches a period and
.B \e/
a slash.
In a constant,
.B \eb
is a backspace.
.PP
Where a regular expression is expected, any other expression is
converted to a string and compiled as a dynamic regular expression.
In a string constant, the backslashes must be doubled, as in
.BR \(dq\e\e.\(dq .
A string that is not a valid extended regular expression is compiled as
a basic regular expression.
.PP
While
.B IGNORECASE
is true, matching ignores the case of ASCII letters.
.SS Records and fields
Records are separated by
.BR RS :
.IP \(bu 2
A single character, newline by default, separates records.
.IP \(bu 2
An empty string separates records at one or more blank lines, and makes
newline a field separator in addition to a single-character or regular
expression
.BR FS .
With a regular expression,
.B NF
is then wrong; see
.BR NOTES .
Leading newlines in the input are skipped.
.IP \(bu 2
A longer string is a regular expression.
.PP
Each record is
.BR $0 ,
and is split into the fields
.BR $1 ,
.BR $2 ,
\&... through
.BI $ NF
by
.BR FS :
.IP \(bu 2
A single space, the default, separates fields at runs of spaces, tabs,
and newlines, and ignores those at the start and end.
.IP \(bu 2
Any other single character separates fields literally, even one special
in regular expressions, such as
.BR | .
While
.B IGNORECASE
is true, a letter matches either case.
.IP \(bu 2
An empty string makes each byte a field.
.IP \(bu 2
A longer string is a regular expression.
.PP
An assignment to
.B FS
applies from the next record.
A reference to a field beyond
.B NF
gives the empty string.
Assigning to a field, or to
.BR NF ,
rebuilds
.B $0
from the fields joined by
.BR OFS ,
and assigning to a field beyond
.B NF
first adds empty fields.
Assigning to
.B $0
splits it again.
A negative field number or
.B NF
is a fatal error.
.SS Statements
.TP
\fBif (\fIcondition\fB) \fIstatement\fR [\fBelse \fIstatement\fR]
Run the first
.I statement
if
.I condition
is true, and otherwise the second, if any.
.TP
\fBwhile (\fIcondition\fB) \fIstatement\fR
Run
.I statement
while
.I condition
is true.
.TP
\fBdo \fIstatement \fBwhile (\fIcondition\fB)\fR
Run
.IR statement ,
then repeat while
.I condition
is true.
.TP
\fBfor (\fIinit\fB; \fIcondition\fB; \fIstep\fB) \fIstatement\fR
Evaluate
.IR init ,
then run
.I statement
and evaluate
.I step
while
.I condition
is true.
Each of the three expressions may be empty, and an empty
.I condition
is true.
.TP
\fBfor (\fIvar \fBin \fIarray\fB) \fIstatement\fR
Run
.I statement
with
.I var
set to each subscript that
.I array
has when the loop starts, in no particular order.
.TP
.BR break ", " continue
Leave the innermost loop, or start its next iteration.
.TP
.B next
Stop processing the current record and read the next.
.TP
.B nextfile
Stop processing the current input file and go on to the next.
.TP
\fBexit\fR [\fIexpr\fR]
Run the
.B END
rules, then exit with status
.IR expr ,
or with the status of an earlier
.BR exit ,
or 0.
In an
.B END
rule, exit immediately.
.TP
\fBreturn\fR [\fIexpr\fR]
Return from a function with the value of
.IR expr ,
or with the empty string, which does not compare equal to 0.
A function that ends without
.B return
also returns the empty string.
.TP
\fBdelete \fIarray\fB[\fIsubscript\fB]\fR
Remove an element.
Without a subscript,
.BI delete " array"
removes them all.
.TP
\fBprint\fR [\fIexpr\fR, ...] [\fIredirection\fR]
Write the values separated by
.B OFS
and followed by
.BR ORS ,
or
.B $0
when there are none.
Numbers are written as described under
.BR Values .
.TP
\fBprintf \fIformat\fR[, \fIexpr\fR, ...] [\fIredirection\fR]
Write the values formatted by
.I format
as described under
.BR Formats .
.PP
An expression may also be a statement, and braces group statements into
one.
The values of
.B print
and
.B printf
may be enclosed in parentheses, and outside parentheses a
.B >
is a redirection:
.TP
.BI > " file"
Write to
.IR file ,
truncating it when it is first opened.
It stays open, so later output to it follows.
.TP
.BI >> " file"
Append to
.IR file .
.TP
.BI | " command"
Write to the standard input of
.IR command ,
which runs in
.BR "sh\ \-c" .
.PP
Each distinct
.I file
or
.I command
is opened once and stays open until closed with
.BR close .
At exit,
.B awk
closes every pipe and waits for its command.
The names
.BR /dev/stdin ,
.BR /dev/stdout ,
and
.B /dev/stderr
refer to the standard streams.
.SS Input
.B getline
reads one record at a time, from the main input or from a file or
command named in the expression:
.TP
.B getline
Read the next record of the main input into
.BR $0 ,
and set
.BR NF ,
.BR NR ,
.BR FNR ,
and
.BR RT .
.TP
.BI "getline " var
Read the next record of the main input into
.IR var ,
and set
.BR NR ,
.BR FNR ,
and
.BR RT .
.TP
.BI "getline < " file
Read the next record of
.I file
into
.BR $0 ,
and set
.B NF
and
.BR RT .
.TP
.BI "getline " var " < " file
Read the next record of
.I file
into
.IR var ,
and set
.BR RT .
.TP
.IB command " | getline"
Read the next record of the output of
.IR command ,
which runs in
.BR "sh\ \-c" ,
into
.BR $0 ,
and set
.B NF
and
.BR RT .
.TP
.IB command " | getline " var
Read the next record of the output of
.I command
into
.IR var ,
and set
.BR RT .
.PP
.B getline
returns 1 after reading a record, 0 at the end of the input, and \-1 if
.I file
cannot be opened or read, setting
.BR ERRNO .
A file or command stays open, and successive calls read successive
records, until it is closed with
.BR close .
.SS Formats
.B printf
and
.B sprintf
copy their format to the output, replacing each conversion specification
with the next value.
A specification is a
.BR % ,
optional flags
.BR \- ,
.BR + ,
space,
.BR # ,
and
.BR 0 ,
an optional width, an optional precision after a period, and a
conversion character.
A width or precision of
.B *
takes the next value, before the one converted, as an integer.
A negative width from
.B *
left-justifies with
.B c
and
.BR s ,
and is ignored with the other conversions.
A negative precision from
.B *
is ignored.
The conversion characters are:
.TP
.B c
A number as the byte with that value, modulo 256, or the first byte of
a string.
.TP
.BR d ", " i ", " u
The value truncated toward zero, in decimal, as
.B %.0f
writes it, so it may be larger than any C integer type.
With
.BR u ,
a negative value is instead converted to a 64-bit unsigned integer, as
with
.BR x .
A precision is ignored, and the
.B #
flag adds a decimal point.
.TP
.BR o ", " x ", " X
The value truncated toward zero and converted to a 64-bit unsigned
integer, so that a negative value gives its two's complement, in octal
or hexadecimal.
A precision is ignored.
.TP
.BR e ", " E ", " f ", " F ", " g ", " G ", " a ", " A
The value as a floating-point number, as in C, except that
.B F
prints nothing.
.TP
.B s
The value as a string.
.TP
.B %
A literal
.BR % ,
consuming no value.
.PP
A missing value is the empty string or 0.
Any other letter ends the specification, which is written unchanged and
consumes no value, so length modifiers such as
.B l
are not supported.
.SS Functions
Arithmetic:
.TP
.BI atan2( y ", " x )
The arctangent of
.IR y / x
in radians, from \-pi to pi.
.TP
\fBcos(\fIx\fB)\fR, \fBsin(\fIx\fB)\fR
The cosine and sine of
.I x
radians.
.TP
\fBexp(\fIx\fB)\fR, \fBlog(\fIx\fB)\fR
The exponential and natural logarithm.
.TP
.BI int( x )
.I x
truncated toward zero.
.TP
.B rand()
A random number from 0 up to, but not including, 1.
The sequence is the same in every run until
.B srand
is called.
.TP
.BI sqrt( x )
The square root.
.TP
\fBsrand(\fR[\fIx\fR]\fB)\fR
Seed
.B rand
with
.IR x ,
or with the current time in seconds, and return the previous seed,
initially 1.
.PP
Strings:
.TP
\fBgensub(\fIr\fB, \fIs\fB, \fIh\fR [\fB, \fIt\fR]\fB)\fR
Return a copy of
.IR t ,
or of
.BR $0 ,
in which matches of the regular expression
.I r
are replaced by
.IR s ;
.I t
is not changed.
If
.I h
is a positive number, only match number
.I h
is replaced, and otherwise, as with
.BR \(dqg\(dq ,
every match.
In
.IR s ,
.B &
and
.B \e0
stand for the matched text,
.B \e1
through
.B \e9
for the text matched by that parenthesized subexpression, and
.B \e&
for a literal
.BR & .
.TP
\fBgsub(\fIr\fB, \fIs\fR [\fB, \fIt\fR]\fB)\fR
Replace every match of
.I r
in the variable, field, or element
.IR t ,
or in
.BR $0 ,
by
.IR s ,
and return the number of replacements.
In
.IR s ,
.B &
stands for the matched text.
Before an
.BR & ,
each pair of backslashes stands for one backslash, and a backslash left
over makes the
.B &
literal, so
.B \e&
is a literal
.B &
and
.B \e\e&
is a backslash followed by the matched text.
Elsewhere, runs of up to three backslashes are copied as they are, and
longer runs may be shortened.
.TP
.BI index( s ", " t )
The position of the first occurrence of
.I t
in
.IR s ,
counting from 1, or 0 if there is none or
.I t
is empty.
.TP
\fBlength\fR[\fB(\fIs\fB)\fR]
The length of
.IR s ,
or of
.B $0
without an argument.
For an array, the number of elements.
.TP
.BI match( s ", " r )
The position of the leftmost longest match of
.I r
in
.IR s ,
or 0.
Set
.B RSTART
to the position and
.B RLENGTH
to the length of the match, or \-1.
.TP
\fBsplit(\fIs\fB, \fIa\fR [\fB, \fIfs\fR]\fB)\fR
Delete the elements of array
.IR a ,
split
.I s
into
\fIa\fB[1]\fR
through
\fIa\fB[\fIn\fB]\fR
by
.IR fs ,
or by
.BR FS ,
and return
.IR n .
.I fs
is interpreted as
.B FS
is, and may be a regular expression constant.
.TP
\fBsprintf(\fIformat\fR [\fB, \fIexpr\fR ...]\fB)\fR
The string that
.B printf
would write.
.TP
\fBsub(\fIr\fB, \fIs\fR [\fB, \fIt\fR]\fB)\fR
Like
.BR gsub ,
for the first match only.
.TP
\fBsubstr(\fIs\fB, \fIm\fR [\fB, \fIn\fR]\fB)\fR
Up to
.I n
bytes of
.I s
starting at position
.IR m ,
or the rest of
.IR s .
Fractions in
.I m
and
.I n
are truncated, and an
.I m
before the start counts as 1 without shortening the result.
.TP
\fBtolower(\fIs\fB)\fR, \fBtoupper(\fIs\fB)\fR
.I s
with ASCII letters converted to lowercase or uppercase.
.PP
Bit operations, on 32-bit unsigned integers:
.TP
.BI and( a ", " b )
Bitwise and.
.TP
.BI compl( a )
Bitwise complement.
.TP
\fBlshift(\fIa\fB, \fIn\fB)\fR, \fBrshift(\fIa\fB, \fIn\fB)\fR
.I a
shifted left or right by
.I n
bits.
.TP
.BI or( a ", " b )
Bitwise or.
.TP
.BI xor( a ", " b )
Bitwise exclusive or.
.PP
Time:
.TP
.BI mktime( spec )
The time in seconds since 1970-01-01 00:00:00 UTC of the local time
given by the string
.IR spec ,
\(dq\fIYYYY MM DD HH MM SS\fR\(dq.
An optional seventh number is positive for daylight saving time, 0 for
standard time, or negative to determine it.
Out-of-range values are normalized, so month 13 is January of the next
year.
The result is \-1 when
.I spec
has fewer than six numbers or a month of 0, or gives a time before the
epoch.
.TP
\fBstrftime(\fR[\fIformat\fR [\fB, \fIt\fR]]\fB)\fR
The time
.IR t ,
in seconds since the epoch, or the current time, formatted in local time
by the C function
.BR strftime .
The default
.I format
is
.BR "\(dq%a %b %d %H:%M:%S %Z %Y\(dq" .
A result longer than 239 bytes is the empty string.
See
.B NOTES
for the conversions.
.TP
.B systime()
The current time in seconds since the epoch.
.PP
Input and output:
.TP
.BI close( name )
Close the file or pipe that
.I name
opened, so that the next use of
.I name
opens it again.
Return 0 on success, or \-1 and set
.B ERRNO
if
.I name
is not open.
.TP
\fBfflush(\fR[\fIname\fR]\fB)\fR
Flush standard output, or the output file or pipe
.IR name ,
or all output if
.I name
is the empty string.
.TP
.BI system( command )
Flush all output, run
.I command
in
.BR "sh\ \-c" ,
wait for it, and return its exit status, from 0 to 255.
An empty
.I command
returns 0 without running anything.
.SS Variables
.TP
.B ARGC
The number of elements in
.BR ARGV .
.TP
.B ARGIND
The index in
.B ARGV
of the current input file.
.TP
.B ARGV
.B ARGV[0]
is
.BR awk ,
and
.B ARGV[1]
through
.B ARGV[ARGC\-1]
are the operands after the program.
Changes made before the input is read change the input files, and empty
elements are skipped.
.TP
.B CONVFMT
The format for converting non-integral numbers to strings, by default
.BR %.6g .
It holds one conversion specification, as for
.BR printf ,
and any other text, with
.B %%
for a literal
.BR % .
The conversion is
.BR e ,
.BR E ,
.BR f ,
.BR F ,
.BR g ,
.BR G ,
.BR a ,
or
.BR A ;
.BR d ,
.BR i ,
.BR o ,
.BR u ,
.BR x ,
or
.BR X ,
which convert the number to a 32-bit
.BR int ;
or
.BR s ,
which writes the number as
.B %.6g
does.
Any other conversion, a width or precision of
.BR * ,
or a second specification is a fatal error when a number is converted.
A format with no specification gives its own text.
.TP
.B ENVIRON
The environment, indexed by variable name.
Unlike Windows environment variable names, the subscripts are
case-sensitive.
Changing it does not affect the commands that
.B awk
runs.
.TP
.B ERRNO
The C
.B errno
value from the last failed
.B getline
or
.BR close ,
a number rather than a message.
.TP
.B FILENAME
The name of the current input file,
.B \-
for standard input, or empty in
.BR BEGIN .
.TP
.B FNR
The number of the current record within the current file.
.TP
.B FS
The field separator, by default a single space.
.TP
.B IGNORECASE
When true, regular expression matching, string comparison,
.BR index ,
and single-character field separators ignore the case of ASCII letters.
.TP
.B NF
The number of fields in the current record.
.TP
.B NR
The number of records read so far.
.TP
.B OFMT
The format for non-integral numbers written by
.BR print ,
by default
.BR %.6g .
It is interpreted as
.B CONVFMT
is.
.TP
.B OFS
The output field separator, by default a single space.
.TP
.B ORS
The output record separator, by default a newline.
.TP
.B RLENGTH
The length of the text matched by
.BR match ,
or \-1.
.TP
.B RS
The record separator, by default a newline.
.TP
.B RSTART
The position of the text matched by
.BR match ,
or 0.
.TP
.B RT
The text that ended the last record read, such as the newline or the
text that a regular expression
.B RS
matched.
.TP
.B SUBSEP
The separator of multiple subscripts, by default
.BR \(dq\e034\(dq .
.SH OPTIONS
Options must come before the program and the other operands, and option
parsing stops at the first operand or at
.BR \-\- .
An option's argument may be attached, as in
.BR \-F: ,
or be the next argument.
When
.BR \-f ,
.BR \-e ,
or
.B \-E
gives the program, the first operand is an input file.
.TP
\fB\-v \fIvar\fB=\fIvalue\fR
Assign
.I value
to the variable
.I var
before the program runs.
.TP
.BI "\-F " fs
Set
.B FS
to
.IR fs ,
after processing escapes, so
.B \-F \(aq\et\(aq
gives a tab.
.TP
.BI "\-f " progfile
Read the program from
.IR progfile ,
where
.B \-
means standard input.
.TP
.BI "\-E " progfile
Read the program from
.I progfile
as
.B \-f
does, then stop option parsing.
Every remaining operand is an input file, even one of the form
\fIvar\fB=\fIvalue\fR.
.TP
.BI "\-e " text
Use
.I text
as program text.
.TP
.BI "\-W " option
Ignored, with a warning.
.TP
.B \-\-help
As the first argument, print a usage message on standard output and
exit with status 0.
.PP
.B \-f
and
.B \-e
may be given several times and mixed, and their programs are combined
in order.
Each must be complete on its own, but functions are shared, so one can
be a library of functions for the others.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BB_GLOBBING
Unless it is
.BR 0 ,
the C runtime replaces each argument containing wildcards with the names
of the files it matches, if any, even when the argument is quoted.
From
.BR cmd.exe ,
a program containing
.B *
or
.B ?\&
is replaced if it happens to match file names.
When it is unset, busybox sets it to
.B 0
for its child processes, so commands run from the busybox shell, which
expands wildcards itself, receive their arguments as given.
.TP
.BR LC_ALL ", " LC_CTYPE ", " LANG
If the first of these that is set has the value
.BR C ,
output to a console is not converted, as described in
.BR NOTES ,
and the console interprets the bytes in its own code page.
.TP
.B TZ
The time zone of
.B mktime
and
.BR strftime ,
in the format of the Microsoft C runtime, such as
.B UTC0
or
.BR EST5EDT .
When it is unset, the Windows time zone setting applies.
.PP
.B awk
copies the whole environment into
.BR ENVIRON ,
and the commands it runs inherit it.
Variables that affect every busybox applet are described in
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Invalid usage, a syntax error, or a fatal error, such as division by
zero or a file that cannot be opened.
.PP
An
.B exit
statement with an expression sets the status to its value converted to
an integer, modulo 256.
.PP
Errors in the program are reported on standard error as
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
awk: \fIsource\fR:\fIline\fR: \fImessage\fR
.EE
.RE
.PP
where
.I source
is the
.B \-f
file name, or
.B cmd. line
for program text from the command line.
.SH EXAMPLES
Print the first field of the rows of a CSV file whose second field is at
least 100:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ cat stock.csv
name,qty
bolt,120
nut,80
washer,300
$ awk \-F, \(aqNR > 1 && $2 >= 100 { print $1 }\(aq stock.csv
bolt
washer
.EE
.RE
.PP
Total the sizes in a long directory listing:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ ls \-l | awk \(aq{ s += $5 } END { print s }\(aq
.EE
.RE
.PP
List the symbols that an object file needs from elsewhere:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ nm \-u main.o | awk \(aq{ print $2 }\(aq
__main
puts
.EE
.RE
.PP
Show where lines are longer than 72 bytes:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ awk \(aqlength > 72 { print FILENAME ":" FNR }\(aq *.c
.EE
.RE
.PP
Count the words in a file, most frequent first:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ awk \(aq{ for (i = 1; i <= NF; i++) n[$i]++ }
    END { for (w in n) print n[w], w }\(aq notes.txt | sort \-rn
.EE
.RE
.PP
Pass a value from the shell, compared as a number:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ awk \-v max="$LIMIT" \(aq$2 > max\(aq data.txt
.EE
.RE
.PP
Combine a library of functions with a program on the command line:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ awk \-f lib.awk \-e \(aq{ print trim($0) }\(aq input.txt
.EE
.RE
.PP
Print the date:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ awk \(aqBEGIN { print strftime("%Y\-%m\-%d") }\(aq
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Input files, standard input, program files, and the output of commands
read by
.B getline
are read in text mode: CR LF becomes LF, and a Ctrl-Z byte ends the
input.
Program text on the command line is not, and a CR in it is a syntax
error.
Output has bare LF line endings, and is flushed after every
.B print
and
.B printf
statement.
.PP
Commands run by
.BR system ,
.BR "print |" ,
and
.B "| getline"
run in busybox
.BR "sh\ \-c" ,
not
.BR cmd.exe .
.B close
of a pipe returns 0 once its command finishes, not the command's exit
status.
A command that cannot be found is reported by the shell, so
.B "| getline"
sees the end of input rather than returning \-1.
.PP
File names may use drive letters and either kind of slash.
Besides
.BR /dev/stdin ,
.BR /dev/stdout ,
and
.BR /dev/stderr ,
the name
.B /dev/null
works, and
.B /dev/tty
is the console.
As an operand or as the argument of
.BR \-f ,
.B \-
means standard input, but after
.B <
or
.B >
it names a file, so use
.B /dev/stdin
or
.B /dev/stdout
there.
.PP
Strings are byte strings.
.BR length ,
.BR substr ,
.BR index ,
.BR match ,
and
.B printf
widths count bytes, so a non-ASCII UTF-8 character counts as two to
four.
In a regular expression,
.B .\&
and bracket expressions match single bytes.
On Windows 10 version 1903 and later, busybox runs with UTF-8 as its code
page, so program text and file names from the command line are UTF-8.
On earlier versions they are in the ANSI code page.
When standard output is a console, output is converted from that code
page to the console's for display.
Strings cannot contain NUL bytes, and with a single-character
.BR RS ,
a NUL byte in the input also ends a record.
.PP
Numbers are formatted by the Microsoft C runtime, so exponents have
three digits, as in
.BR 1e+020 ;
infinity and NaN are written as
.BR 1.#INF ,
.BR \-1.#INF ,
.BR 1.#QNAN ,
or
.BR \-1.#IND ;
and the
.B %F
conversion prints nothing.
Strings such as
.BR 0x1A ,
.BR inf ,
and
.B nan
are numeric, so input fields like them compare as numbers.
.PP
Integral numbers convert to strings as
.B %.0f
writes them, and so do the
.BR d ,
.BR i ,
and
.B u
conversions of
.BR printf .
This gives at most 17 significant digits, followed by zeros, as in
.BR 18446744073709552000 .
Infinity and NaN are not integral, and the integer conversions write
them as
.B %g
does.
Negative zero converts to
.BR 0 ,
so
.B printf \(dq%d\(dq, \-0.5
writes
.BR 0 .
.PP
.B mktime
returns a 32-bit value, so it wraps around for times after 03:14:07 UTC
on 19 January 2038.
.B strftime
supports the conversions of the Microsoft C runtime, plus
.BR %e ,
.BR %s ,
.BR %T ,
.BR %z ,
and a
.B \-
flag that removes the padding of
.BR d ,
.BR H ,
.BR I ,
.BR j ,
.BR m ,
.BR M ,
.BR S ,
.BR U ,
.BR w ,
.BR W ,
.BR y ,
and
.BR Y ,
as in
.BR %\-d .
A format with any other conversion, such as
.B %F
or
.BR %D ,
gives the empty string.
When
.B TZ
is unset,
.B %Z
gives the Windows name of the time zone, such as
.BR "Eastern Standard Time" .
.B strftime
treats a time before the epoch or after the year 3000 as 0.
.PP
Differences from POSIX awk and gawk:
.IP \(bu 2
.B ^
and
.B **
group left to right, so
.B 2^3^2
is 64.
.IP \(bu 2
An assignment in either branch of
.B ?:
is a syntax error unless it is in parentheses, as in
.BR "c ? (a = 1) : (b = 2)" .
.IP \(bu 2
.IB command " | getline"
does not change
.B NR
or
.BR FNR .
.IP \(bu 2
.B substr
truncates fractional positions and lengths instead of rounding them.
.IP \(bu 2
An input file that cannot be opened is a fatal error, and the
.B END
rules do not run.
.IP \(bu 2
With an empty
.BR RS ,
a last record followed by a single newline keeps that newline, which
adds an empty field when
.B FS
is a single character other than space.
.IP \(bu 2
With an empty
.B RS
and a regular expression
.BR FS ,
newlines in a record separate fields but are not counted in
.BR NF ,
so the fields at the end of the record are lost.
.IP \(bu 2
.B ERRNO
is a number.
.IP \(bu 2
There are no long options other than
.BR \-\-help ,
and
.B \-W
is ignored.
.PP
Extensions to POSIX awk, all from gawk, include
.BR \-e ,
.BR \-E ,
.BR ARGIND ,
.BR ERRNO ,
.BR IGNORECASE ,
.BR RT ,
regular expression
.BR RS ,
.BR func ,
.BR ** ,
.BR **= ,
.B length
of an array,
.BR gensub ,
the bit and time functions, and hexadecimal and octal constants.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR cut (1),
.BR grep (1),
.BR sed (1),
.BR sh (1),
.BR sort (1)
